<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/18th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty · Peace</p><h1 class="enc-title">Peace of Szatmár</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1711">1711</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Treaty of Szatmár · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rakoczis-war-of-independence">Rákóczi's War of Independence</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Peace of Szatmár was concluded in April 1711 between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> monarchy and the Hungarian insurgents of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-ii-rakoczi">Francis II Rákóczi</a>, ending the war of independence he had led since 1703. The rebels received an amnesty and the Habsburgs promised to respect the constitution and religious liberties of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, while Rákóczi himself rejected the terms and went into exile.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/peace-of-szatmar.jpg" alt="Peace of Szatmár" width="320" height="255" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Österreichisches Staatsarchiv · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Friede_von_Sathmar_1711_01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-turkish-war">Great Turkish War</a> the forces of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> monarchy conquered large parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-hungary">Ottoman Hungary</a>, but the new rulers soon met resistance from Hungarian magnates led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-ii-rakoczi">Francis II Rákóczi</a>. From 1703 his rebellion spread through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-hungary">Upper Hungary</a>, today mostly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovakia">Slovakia</a>, as well as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transylvania">Transylvania</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carpathian-ruthenia">Carpathian Ruthenia</a>. In the 1708 Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trencin">Trencsén</a> a Habsburg army under Field Marshal Sigbert Heister, backed by Rascian forces, decisively defeated the rebels, yet the conflict dragged on.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-eugene-of-savoy">Prince Eugene of Savoy</a>, president of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aulic-war-council">Hofkriegsrat</a>, appointed the loyal Hungarian Field Marshal János Pálffy as chief negotiator. In November 1710 Pálffy contacted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuruc">Kuruc</a> commander Sándor Károlyi, and a truce followed on 13 January 1711.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Pálffy and Rákóczi met at Vaja on 31 January, but Rákóczi refused the conditions offered. On 21 February he left for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> to seek help from the envoys of Tsar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-the-great">Peter the Great</a>, who was occupied with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-northern-war">Great Northern War</a>. He named Károlyi commander-in-chief of the remaining <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuruc">Kuruc</a> strongholds and forbade further talks. Károlyi ignored the order and called an assembly of the insurgents at Szatmár, which on 4 April agreed preliminary conditions and ordered a ceasefire.</p><p>On 17 April Emperor Joseph I died. His brother and successor, Charles VI, needed an end to the fighting in order to secure the Hungarian crown, and his envoys soon reached agreement at Szatmár, the present-day Satu Mare in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a>. Rákóczi appeared there and refused all concessions, but he could no longer prevail and returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>. The treaty was concluded on 29 April, and Pálffy, Károlyi and numerous Imperial, Hungarian, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuruc">Kuruc</a> and Transylvanian envoys held a signing ceremony on 1 May 1711.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>The accord granted a full royal pardon to the leaders and rank and file of the insurrection alike. The nobility kept their ancestral privileges on condition of an oath of fealty to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-habsburg">House of Habsburg</a>, and the peasantry kept their existing liberties. The Crown promised free exercise of religion, the constitutional integrity of both the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-hungary">Kingdom of Hungary</a> and the Principality of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transylvania">Transylvania</a>, and the regular summoning of parliament, and it suppressed new foreign institutions judged harmful to the Hungarian estates. On 1 May 1711 some 12,000 former followers of Rákóczi swore allegiance to the House of Habsburg in the fields outside Majtény.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Treaty of Szatmár” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After the <b>Peace of Szatmár</b> in 1711 ended Francis II Rákóczi's revolt, Transylvania was governed directly by the Habsburgs as a separate land of the Hungarian Crown, under the Leopoldine Diploma of 1691.</blockquote><a href="/state/grand-principality-of-transylvania">Grand Principality of Transylvania</a></li><li><blockquote>The failure of Francis II Rákóczi's revolt ended at the <b>Peace of Szatmár</b> in 1711, leading to the Grand Principality of Transylvania.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-transylvania">Principality of Transylvania</a></li></ul></section>
